
---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Configuration Reference"
  description="A complete reference for Loot Containers configuration files, settings, defaults, and validation rules."
>
  <div class="doc-section">
    <span class="eyebrow">CONFIGURATION</span>
    <h1>Configuration Reference</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Learn how Loot Containers stores its configuration, what each setting
      controls, and how to customize loot tables, rarities, reward odds, and
      container assignments.
    </p>

    <p>
      Configuration files are stored in your Minecraft configuration
      directory. Loot Containers creates its configuration folder and default
      files automatically when they are missing.
    </p>

    <div class="info-card">
      <strong>Configuration folder</strong>
      <pre><code>config/lootcontainers/</code></pre>
      <p>
        On Windows, this folder is located inside your Minecraft instance
        directory.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="doc-section">
    <h2>Configuration files</h2>
    <p>
      Loot Containers uses separate files for general settings, rarity
      definitions, loot tables, and container assignments.
    </p>

    <div class="table-wrapper">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>File</th>
            <th>Purpose</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><code>settings.json</code></td>
            <td>General settings and stored permission options.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>rarities.json</code></td>
            <td>Rarity names, IDs, colors, descriptions, and weights.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>loot_tables/loot_tables.json</code></td>
            <td>Loot table definitions, item pools, selection systems, and odds.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>loot_tables/container_assignments.json</code></td>
            <td>Rules connecting blocks and dimensions to loot tables.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      All configuration files use JSON and are saved with pretty formatting
      to make manual editing easier.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="doc-section">
    <h2>General settings</h2>
    <p>
      General settings are stored in <code>settings.json</code>. These settings
      contain default values for reward behavior, notifications, and
      configuration permissions.
    </p>

    <h3>Default configuration</h3>
    <pre><code>{`{
  "defaultRewardChance": 0.25,
  "enableRewardNotifications": true,
  "requireOp": true,
  "allowlistEnabled": false,
  "allowOpBypass": true,
  "allowedPlayers": []
}`}</code></pre>

    <h3>Setting reference</h3>
    <div class="table-wrapper">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Setting</th>
            <th>Default</th>
            <th>Description</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><code>defaultRewardChance</code></td>
            <td><code>0.25</code></td>
            <td>
              Reserved for a future default reward chance feature. Not
              implemented yet.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>enableRewardNotifications</code></td>
            <td><code>true</code></td>
            <td>
              Reserved for future reward notification controls. Not
              implemented yet.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>requireOp</code></td>
            <td><code>true</code></td>
            <td>
              Stored permission option. Not currently used by server-side
              authorization.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>allowlistEnabled</code></td>
            <td><code>false</code></td>
            <td>
              Stored option for enabling an allowlist. Allowlist enforcement
              is not implemented yet.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>allowOpBypass</code></td>
            <td><code>true</code></td>
            <td>
              Stored permission option. Not currently used by server-side
              authorization.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>allowedPlayers</code></td>
            <td><code>[]</code></td>
            <td>
              Stores player entries for the allowlist interface. Entries
              are not currently enforced by server authorization.
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="info-card">
      <strong>Implementation status</strong>
      <p>
        The general settings file is saved and loaded, but not every setting
        currently affects gameplay. The reward chance and notification
        settings are reserved for future functionality. The permission
        options are stored by the configuration screen, but do not replace
        the current server authorization checks.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h3>Current server authorization</h3>
    <p>
      Server-side configuration synchronization and saving currently check
      whether a player has permission level 2 or higher. The stored
      permission options above do not change that check.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="doc-section">
    <h2>Rarity configuration</h2>
    <p>
      Rarity definitions are stored in
      <code>loot_tables/../rarities.json</code>, which resolves to
      <code>config/lootcontainers/rarities.json</code>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Each rarity has an ID, display name, color, description, and selection
      weight. Rarity weights determine how likely each rarity is to be
      selected when a loot table uses the RARITY selection system.
    </p>

    <h3>Default rarities</h3>
    <div class="table-wrapper">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Rarity</th>
            <th>ID</th>
            <th>Default color</th>
            <th>Weight</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Common</td>
            <td><code>common</code></td>
            <td><code>#55FF55</code></td>
            <td>60.0</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Uncommon</td>
            <td><code>uncommon</code></td>
            <td><code>#AAFF55</code></td>
            <td>25.0</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Rare</td>
            <td><code>rare</code></td>
            <td><code>#55AAFF</code></td>
            <td>10.0</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Epic</td>
            <td><code>epic</code></td>
            <td><code>#AA55FF</code></td>
            <td>4.0</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Legendary</td>
            <td><code>legendary</code></td>
            <td><code>#FFAA00</code></td>
            <td>1.0</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Mythic</td>
            <td><code>mythic</code></td>
            <td><code>#35CFFF</code></td>
            <td>0.1</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      The default rarity weights add up to 100.1. These are relative weights,
      not guaranteed percentages.
    </p>

    <h3>Rarity properties</h3>
    <div class="table-wrapper">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Property</th>
            <th>Default</th>
            <th>Requirements</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><code>id</code></td>
            <td><code>common</code></td>
            <td>
              Required. Lowercase letters, numbers, underscores, and hyphens.
              Must be unique.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>displayName</code></td>
            <td><code>Common</code></td>
            <td>Required and cannot be blank.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>color</code></td>
            <td><code>#55FF55</code></td>
            <td>Must be a six-digit hexadecimal color beginning with #.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>description</code></td>
            <td><code>""</code></td>
            <td>Optional. Can be blank.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>weight</code></td>
            <td><code>60.0</code></td>
            <td>Must be finite and greater than or equal to 0.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Rarity validation</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>At least one rarity must exist.</li>
      <li>Rarity IDs must be unique, ignoring capitalization.</li>
      <li>At least one rarity must have a positive weight.</li>
      <li>Zero-weight rarities are valid but cannot be selected.</li>
      <li>Negative, infinite, and NaN weights are invalid.</li>
      <li>There is no configured upper limit for rarity weights.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="info-card">
      <strong>Missing rarity definitions</strong>
      <p>
        When a valid rarity configuration is loaded, missing built-in rarity
        entries are added automatically. Existing entries are retained.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="doc-section">
    <h2>Loot table configuration</h2>
    <p>
      Loot tables are stored in
      <code>config/lootcontainers/loot_tables/loot_tables.json</code>.
      Each table defines its item pool, selection method, and reward chance
      system.
    </p>

    <h3>Default loot table</h3>
    <p>
      The default table created by the mod is named
      <code>overworld</code> and has the display name
      <strong>Overworld Rewards</strong>.
    </p>

    <div class="table-wrapper">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Property</th>
            <th>Default value</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><code>id</code></td>
            <td><code>overworld</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>displayName</code></td>
            <td><code>Overworld Rewards</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>description</code></td>
            <td>Starter rewards for overworld containers.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>chanceSystem</code></td>
            <td><code>FIXED</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>selectionSystem</code></td>
            <td><code>RARITY</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>rewardChance</code></td>
            <td><code>0.25</code></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Loot table properties</h3>
    <div class="table-wrapper">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Property</th>
            <th>Default</th>
            <th>Requirements</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><code>id</code></td>
            <td><code>default</code></td>
            <td>
              Required. Lowercase letters, numbers, underscores, and hyphens.
              Must be unique.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>displayName</code></td>
            <td><code>Default Loot Table</code></td>
            <td>Required and cannot be blank.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>description</code></td>
            <td><code>""</code></td>
            <td>Optional description.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>chanceSystem</code></td>
            <td><code>FIXED</code></td>
            <td><code>FIXED</code> or <code>PROGRESSIVE</code>.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>selectionSystem</code></td>
            <td><code>RARITY</code></td>
            <td><code>RARITY</code> or <code>INDIVIDUAL</code>.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>rewardChance</code></td>
            <td><code>0.25</code></td>
            <td>Must be between 0 and 1, inclusive. Not implemented in runtime reward calculations yet.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>items</code></td>
            <td>Default item pool</td>
            <td>Required. Must contain at least one valid item with a positive weight.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>fixedOdds</code></td>
            <td>Default fixed odds</td>
            <td>Required and must pass fixed odds validation.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>progressiveOdds</code></td>
            <td>Default progressive odds</td>
            <td>Required and must pass progressive odds validation.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="info-card">
      <strong>About rewardChance</strong>
      <p>
        Although <code>rewardChance</code> is present in the loot table
        configuration and is validated, it is not currently used by the
        reward generator. Actual reward rolls use the selected
        <code>fixedOdds</code> or <code>progressiveOdds</code> settings.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="doc-section">
    <h2>Loot item properties</h2>
    <p>
      Each loot table contains an <code>items</code> list. Every entry
      represents a possible reward.
    </p>

    <h3>Item properties</h3>
    <div class="table-wrapper">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Property</th>
            <th>Default</th>
            <th>Description and validation</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><code>itemId</code></td>
            <td><code>minecraft:diamond</code></td>
            <td>
              Minecraft item registry ID. Must use a valid namespace and
              item path.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>rarityId</code></td>
            <td><code>common</code></td>
            <td>
              Rarity assigned to this item. Must reference an existing
              rarity when using RARITY selection.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>weight</code></td>
            <td><code>1.0</code></td>
            <td>
              Item selection weight. Must be finite and greater than or
              equal to 0.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>minCount</code></td>
            <td><code>1</code></td>
            <td>Minimum stack size. Must be at least 1.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>maxCount</code></td>
            <td><code>1</code></td>
            <td>
              Maximum stack size. Must be at least 1 and cannot be smaller
              than <code>minCount</code>.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>displayName</code></td>
            <td><code>""</code></td>
            <td>
              Optional display name field. Custom item naming is not
              currently applied by the item builder.
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Default item pool</h3>
    <div class="table-wrapper">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Item</th>
            <th>Rarity</th>
            <th>Weight</th>
            <th>Count range</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><code>minecraft:diamond</code></td>
            <td>Common</td>
            <td>10.0</td>
            <td>1–2</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>minecraft:emerald</code></td>
            <td>Uncommon</td>
            <td>7.0</td>
            <td>1–3</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>minecraft:diamond_sword</code></td>
            <td>Rare</td>
            <td>4.0</td>
            <td>1</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>minecraft:netherite_ingot</code></td>
            <td>Legendary</td>
            <td>1.0</td>
            <td>1</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>minecraft:nether_star</code></td>
            <td>Mythic</td>
            <td>0.1</td>
            <td>1</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Item validation</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Item IDs must contain a valid namespace and resource path.</li>
      <li>Item weights must be finite and cannot be negative.</li>
      <li>At least one item must have a positive weight.</li>
      <li>Minimum and maximum counts must both be at least 1.</li>
      <li>Minimum count cannot exceed maximum count.</li>
      <li>There is no configured maximum item weight or stack count.</li>
      <li>Items with zero weight are not selected.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="info-card">
      <strong>Item names</strong>
      <p>
        The <code>displayName</code> field is accepted by the configuration
        validator, but the current item builder does not apply it to the
        resulting item stack.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="doc-section">
    <h2>Selection systems</h2>
    <p>
      Loot tables support two selection systems. The selected system determines
      how the item pool is evaluated before the reward chance is rolled.
    </p>

    <h3>RARITY</h3>
    <p>
      The RARITY system uses two weighted selection stages:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>Select a rarity using the configured rarity weights.</li>
      <li>Select an item from that rarity using the item weights.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      A rarity is only eligible when it has at least one matching item with
      a finite positive item weight. Rarity weights and item weights serve
      different purposes.
    </p>

    <h3>INDIVIDUAL</h3>
    <p>
      The INDIVIDUAL system places all eligible items into one shared pool.
      It selects an item directly using that item's weight.
    </p>
    <p>
      Rarity weights are ignored by this selection system. An item's
      <code>rarityId</code> may be blank, but if it is provided, it must
      reference an existing rarity.
    </p>

    <div class="info-card">
      <strong>Choosing a selection system</strong>
      <p>
        Use RARITY when you want to control how often rarity tiers appear.
        Use INDIVIDUAL when you want every item to compete directly in one
        weighted pool.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="doc-section">
    <h2>Fixed odds</h2>
    <p>
      Fixed odds give every reward attempt the same chance. They are configured
      inside the loot table's <code>fixedOdds</code> object.
    </p>

    <h3>Default values</h3>
    <pre><code>{`{
  "format": "RATIO",
  "percentage": 1.0,
  "numerator": 1,
  "denominator": 100
}`}</code></pre>

    <h3>Properties</h3>
    <div class="table-wrapper">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Property</th>
            <th>Default</th>
            <th>Description</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><code>format</code></td>
            <td><code>RATIO</code></td>
            <td>Chooses the fixed odds format.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>percentage</code></td>
            <td><code>1.0</code></td>
            <td>Reward chance as a percentage when using PERCENTAGE.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>numerator</code></td>
            <td><code>1</code></td>
            <td>Numerator of the reward chance ratio.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>denominator</code></td>
            <td><code>100</code></td>
            <td>Denominator of the reward chance ratio.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Supported formats</h3>
    <p><strong>RATIO</strong></p>
    <p>
      The reward chance is calculated by dividing the numerator by the
      denominator.
    </p>
    <pre><code>{`{
  "format": "RATIO",
  "numerator": 1,
  "denominator": 100
}`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      This produces a 1% reward chance. The numerator and denominator must
      both be positive, and the numerator cannot exceed the denominator.
    </p>

    <p><strong>PERCENTAGE</strong></p>
    <p>
      The percentage field directly specifies the reward chance.
    </p>
    <pre><code>{`{
  "format": "PERCENTAGE",
  "percentage": 5.0
}`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      This produces a 5% reward chance. The percentage must be between 0
      and 100, inclusive.
    </p>

    <p>
      Only the fields belonging to the selected format affect the calculated
      chance.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="doc-section">
    <h2>Progressive odds and pity</h2>
    <p>
      Progressive odds increase the reward chance after failed attempts.
      This system is commonly called a pity system.
    </p>

    <h3>Default values</h3>
    <pre><code>{`{
  "startingChance": 1.0,
  "increasePerFailure": 1.0,
  "maximumChance": 100.0,
  "failuresBeforeIncrease": 0,
  "resetAfterReward": true
}`}</code></pre>

    <h3>Properties</h3>
    <div class="table-wrapper">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Property</th>
            <th>Default</th>
            <th>Description and validation</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><code>startingChance</code></td>
            <td><code>1.0</code></td>
            <td>Initial reward chance as a percentage. Range: 0–100.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>increasePerFailure</code></td>
            <td><code>1.0</code></td>
            <td>
              Percentage points added for each eligible failure. Must be
              finite and nonnegative.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>maximumChance</code></td>
            <td><code>100.0</code></td>
            <td>Maximum reward chance. Range: 0–100.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>failuresBeforeIncrease</code></td>
            <td><code>0</code></td>
            <td>Number of failed attempts before increases begin. Must be nonnegative.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>resetAfterReward</code></td>
            <td><code>true</code></td>
            <td>Whether the player's failure counter resets after a successful reward.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>How the chance increases</h3>
    <p>
      The chance begins at <code>startingChance</code>. After the configured
      number of initial failures, each additional failure adds
      <code>increasePerFailure</code> percentage points.
    </p>

    <pre><code>{`Chance = startingChance
       + eligibleFailures * increasePerFailure

Chance = minimum(maximumChance, Chance)`}</code></pre>

    <p>
      The final chance is capped by <code>maximumChance</code>. Negative
      failure counts are treated as zero.
    </p>

    <h3>Example using default values</h3>
    <div class="table-wrapper">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Failed attempts</th>
            <th>Reward chance</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>0</td><td>1%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1</td><td>2%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2</td><td>3%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>5</td><td>6%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>10</td><td>11%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>50</td><td>51%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>99</td><td>100%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Failure delay example</h3>
    <p>
      If <code>failuresBeforeIncrease</code> is set to
      <code>3</code>, the initial chance remains unchanged for the first
      four attempts. The chance begins increasing after those failures.
    </p>

    <h3>Pity tracking</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Failure counters are tracked per player UUID and loot table ID.</li>
      <li>Failed progressive reward rolls increase the counter.</li>
      <li>Successful rolls do not increase the counter.</li>
      <li>
        When <code>resetAfterReward</code> is true, a successful reward
        resets the counter for that player and table.
      </li>
      <li>
        When it is false, the existing counter is retained after a reward.
      </li>
      <li>Fixed odds do not use progressive failure tracking.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Pity information is stored using Minecraft's SavedData system and
      synchronized to the player when progressive odds are processed.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="doc-section">
    <h2>Container assignments</h2>
    <p>
      Container assignments connect blocks to loot tables. They are stored
      in <code>config/lootcontainers/loot_tables/container_assignments.json</code>.
    </p>

    <h3>Default assignment</h3>
    <pre><code>{`{
  "id": "overworld_containers",
  "displayName": "Overworld Containers",
  "blockId": "",
  "blockTag": "lootcontainers:loot_containers",
  "lootTableId": "overworld",
  "enabled": true,
  "blockIds": [],
  "dimensionId": ""
}`}</code></pre>

    <h3>Assignment properties</h3>
    <div class="table-wrapper">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Property</th>
            <th>Default</th>
            <th>Description and validation</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><code>id</code></td>
            <td><code>overworld_containers</code></td>
            <td>Required unique identifier. Lowercase letters, numbers, underscores, and hyphens.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>displayName</code></td>
            <td><code>Overworld Containers</code></td>
            <td>Display name for the assignment.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>blockId</code></td>
            <td><code>""</code></td>
            <td>Optional single block registry ID.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>blockTag</code></td>
            <td><code>lootcontainers:loot_containers</code></td>
            <td>Optional block tag used to match multiple blocks.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>blockIds</code></td>
            <td><code>[]</code></td>
            <td>Optional list of individual block registry IDs.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>lootTableId</code></td>
            <td><code>overworld</code></td>
            <td>Required ID of an existing loot table.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>enabled</code></td>
            <td><code>true</code></td>
            <td>Whether the assignment is considered during matching.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>dimensionId</code></td>
            <td><code>""</code></td>
            <td>Optional dimension registry ID. Blank means no dimension restriction.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Block matching methods</h3>
    <p>
      Each assignment must use exactly one block matching method:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>blockId</code> — matches one specific block.</li>
      <li><code>blockIds</code> — matches one of several specific blocks.</li>
      <li><code>blockTag</code> — matches blocks belonging to a tag.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Dimension matching</h3>
    <p>
      A nonempty <code>dimensionId</code> restricts an assignment to that
      dimension. For example, <code>minecraft:overworld</code> matches the
      Overworld dimension.
    </p>

    <h3>Assignment priority</h3>
    <p>
      Assignments are checked in list order. The first enabled assignment
      that matches both the dimension and block is used. If multiple
      assignments match, earlier entries take priority.
    </p>

    <h3>Validation rules</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Assignment IDs must be unique, ignoring capitalization.</li>
      <li>Block IDs and block tags must be valid resource locations.</li>
      <li>Block ID lists cannot contain duplicate entries.</li>
      <li>Exactly one block matching method must be configured.</li>
      <li>The referenced loot table must exist.</li>
      <li>A nonempty dimension ID must be a valid resource location.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="doc-section">
    <h2>How reward attempts work</h2>
    <p>
      When a player interacts with an assigned container, the server
      processes the reward attempt.
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li>The server finds the matching container assignment.</li>
      <li>The container's identity is retrieved or created.</li>
      <li>The server checks whether that player already has a reward result for that container.</li>
      <li>An item is selected using the loot table's selection system.</li>
      <li>The selected item stack is built using its configured count range.</li>
      <li>The configured fixed or progressive reward chance is rolled.</li>
      <li>If successful, the reward is delivered and the result is recorded.</li>
      <li>If unsuccessful, the failed attempt is recorded for progressive pity when applicable.</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="info-card">
      <strong>One attempt per player and container</strong>
      <p>
        Container reward results are tracked using container identities and
        player UUIDs. A recorded result prevents that player from rerolling
        the same container.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p>
      The selected item is built before the reward chance is rolled. If the
      chance fails, that item is discarded and no reward is delivered.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="doc-section">
    <h2>Configuration validation and saving</h2>
    <p>
      Loot Containers validates important configuration data before accepting
      changes.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>Rarity definitions are checked for valid IDs, colors, and weights.</li>
      <li>Loot tables are checked for valid odds, selection systems, and item entries.</li>
      <li>Container assignments are checked for valid matching rules and loot table references.</li>
      <li>Configuration changes submitted through the screen are validated on the server.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Loot table changes are validated before they are saved. The manager
      also restores the previous in-memory configuration if saving fails.
      The server configuration save process keeps backups and attempts to
      roll back files if a save operation fails.
    </p>

    <h3>Editing JSON manually</h3>
    <p>
      If you edit configuration files while the game is running, the current
      in-memory configuration may not immediately reflect those changes.
      Use the configuration screen or reload the relevant configuration
      through the appropriate mod workflow.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="doc-section">
    <h2>Recommended configuration practices</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Make a backup before manually editing configuration files.</li>
      <li>Keep rarity IDs consistent with the IDs referenced by loot items.</li>
      <li>Use positive weights for items and rarities you want to be selectable.</li>
      <li>Use the RARITY system when rarity-level balancing matters.</li>
      <li>Use the INDIVIDUAL system when all items should compete in one pool.</li>
      <li>Test reward odds with a small loot table before applying major changes.</li>
      <li>Check assignment order when multiple rules can match the same block.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

